  "output": "Now I have the full picture. This is a very early-stage Next.js 16 app (mostly landing/onboarding wireframes + one real console page), so most of the categories asked about don't yet have live instances — but there are a few concrete, real issues worth fixing now before the pattern spreads.\n\n## Findings\n\n**Scope note upfront:** this codebase is ~30 files, almost entirely React Server Components with no data-fetching loops, no lists-of-lists, and only one live DB query path (`lib/server/sources.ts`). There are no N+1 query patterns, no client-side caches, and no observed memory leaks yet — the app is too early for those to exist. What follows are the real, actionable items I found.\n\n### 1. Duplicate/redundant DB round-trip — `countSourcesBySide()`\n`lib/server/sources.ts:23-30` calls `listSources()` internally, which does its own `getDb().select()...` + `sourceSchema.parse()` per row. If a future page ever needs both the full list and the counts (e.g. a dashboard that shows \"12 sources configured\" *and* renders the table), you'd pay for two DB round-trips and double the Zod validation for the same rows. Not an N+1, but it's the seed of one, and Server Components make this failure mode easy to miss because a page importing both functions won't dedupe automatically per-request the way `fetch()` does.\n\n**Fix: wrap `listSources` in React's per-request `cache()`** so repeated calls within the same render collapse to one query:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\n\nThis is essentially free insurance — zero behavior change today, and it means `countSourcesBySide()` + `listSources()` called from the same request tree will only hit Postgres once.\n\n### 2. `countSourcesBySide` re-derives counts with three separate `.filter()` passes\nMinor, but it's O(3n) instead of O(n) and reads worse than a single reduce:\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();\n  return all.reduce(\n    (acc, s) => {\n      if (s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\") acc.crm++;\n      else if (s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\") acc.warehouse++;\n      else if (s.kind === \"synthetic\") acc.synthetic++;\n      return acc;\n    },\n    { crm: 0, warehouse: 0, synthetic: 0 },\n  );\n}\n```\n\nAt current source-list sizes (a handful of connectors per tenant) this doesn't matter for wall-clock time; it's a code-quality nit, not a perf bug — skip it unless you're touching this file anyway.\n\n### 3. No index-driven pagination on `listSources`/future `entities`/`matches` queries\nNot a bug today (sources will stay small — a handful of connectors), but `schema.ts` shows `entities`, `matches`, and `conflicts` are the tables that will actually scale (one row per CRM/warehouse record, per match, per field-level conflict — potentially tens of thousands per run). There's no query code against those tables yet, so there's nothing to fix, but **when you write the conflicts/audit list pages, don't reuse the `listSources()` \"select all, filter in JS\" pattern** — use `WHERE`/`LIMIT`/keyset pagination at the DB layer, since `entities_run_source_external_idx` (schema.ts:115) is already there for you to filter by `runId`/`sourceId` instead of loading a run's entire entity set into Node.\n\n### 4. React re-renders / memory leaks: no issues found\n- Every data-bearing page (`sources/page.tsx`, `get-started/page.tsx`) is an `async` Server Component — there's no client-side state, no `useEffect`, no subscriptions, so there's nothing to leak.\n- The only client components (`nav-rail.tsx`, `pipeline-strip.tsx`, `landing-hero.tsx`, `step-list.tsx`, `get-started-hero.tsx`, `sources-empty-state.tsx`, `sign-out-control.tsx`) are either static (Framer Motion entrance animations with no timers/intervals/subscriptions to clean up) or trivial (`usePathname`/`useUser` from Clerk, which manage their own lifecycle).\n- `SourceTable` (`components/sources/source-table.tsx`) maps over a server-fetched array with no local state, no inline arrow-function props causing child re-renders of anything expensive — there's no child component here worth memoizing (`<tr>`/`<td>` aren't components). Premature `useMemo`/`React.memo` here would be pure overhead, not an optimization.\n- The comment in `sources.ts:8-9` explicitly notes \"TanStack Query is reserved for run-status polling only\" — that's the one place a genuine caching/re-render question will show up later (a polling component for run status). Nothing to fix now, but worth flagging: when that polling component lands, set an appropriate `staleTime`/`refetchInterval` and make sure the query key is scoped per-run so switching runs doesn't leave a stale interval polling in the background.\n\n### Bottom line\nThe only concrete change I'd actually make right now is wrapping `listSources()` in `cache()` (item 1) — it's a one-line, zero-risk change that prevents a duplicate-query bug from ever manifesting as you build out more console pages. Everything else is either premature (the app doesn't have the query volume or component complexity yet to need memoization/pagination) or a note for when `entities`/`matches`/`conflicts` queries get written.\n",
